    bottomLine: "The treadmill to buy for supported walking: full-length rails, a very slow start and controls anyone can use.",
    description: "Exerpeutic's Senior Fitness Treadmill is built around the features this page is about. The Amazon listing describes full-length side handrails with soft foam padding and an extra forward rail for more support, a speed range of 0.3 to 4 mph adjusted in 0.1 mph steps, a 43.5 by 16-inch belt, six shock-absorbing deck cushions, a 400 lb maximum user weight, a backlit LCD showing time, distance, calories, speed and pulse, and a console reduced to start, stop and speed up or down. It also has a manual incline for a gentle slope. That combination — rails you can hold the whole way along, a very slow start, small speed steps and controls without menus — is exactly what most people looking for a treadmill with handrails need, and the 400 lb rating leaves a comfortable margin. Its limits are deliberate: 4 mph is a brisk walk, so it is not for running, and the incline is set by hand, with no range given. The listing describes a low step-up height without a figure, so measure or ask the seller if stepping up is a concern. Amazon listed it at $399 on 6 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "redliro-walking-treadmill-for-seniors",
    name: "Redliro Walking Treadmill for Seniors",
    brand: "Redliro",
    category: "Best for Taller Walkers and Small Spaces",
    badge: "Longest Belt",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FX2LY131?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41a2hQ3IOKL._SL500_.jpg","width":425,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $450 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":450,"high":450},
    specs: "Long side rails plus front bar · 0.3 mph start, 0.1 mph steps · 47.6 x 16.5 in. belt · 0–2% incline · 400 lb capacity · folds",
