      "The listing gives no belt size or rail length; check before ordering",
      "Starts at 0.6 mph and tops out at 3.8 mph",
      "A 300 lb rating, the lowest here",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A low-cost way to get long rails and an emergency stop, if the belt size suits you.",
    description: "Yesoul's treadmill for seniors is the least expensive way we found to get rails along the sides of the belt. The Amazon listing describes extended handrails for support and stability, a one-tap emergency button that stops the belt immediately, a safety key that cuts power if you fall back, a six-layer belt with six shock absorbers, a 300 lb maximum user weight, an LED display showing time, calories, steps, speed and distance, a remote control for speed and mode, phone and cup holders, and a speed range of 0.6 to 3.8 mph. The emergency button is a useful addition for a walker who is anxious about stopping quickly, and the remote means you never have to reach for the console. The gaps are in the numbers. The listing does not give the belt size or the length of the rails, and we could not confirm them from a second source, so check the dimensions with the seller before you order, particularly if you are tall. The 0.6 mph start is gentler than most treadmills but less so than the Exerpeutic's 0.3 mph, and 300 lb is the lowest rating here. Amazon listed it at $239.99 on 6 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "sunny-low-deck-treadmill-extended-handrails",
    name: "Sunny Health & Fitness Low Deck Treadmill with Extended Safety Handrails",
    brand: "Sunny Health & Fitness",
