          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill with handrails?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most walkers, the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill: full-length padded side rails and a forward rail, a 0.3 mph start in 0.1 mph steps, a 43.5 by 16-inch belt, a simple console and a 400 lb rating, at about $399 on Amazon on 6 November 2026. The Redliro Walking Treadmill for Seniors gives a longer 47.6-inch belt and folds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are treadmills with long handrails safer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Long side rails give you something to hold along the whole belt, which helps with balance when stepping on and off and while walking. They are one part of safety, alongside a low starting speed, small speed steps, a safety key you always clip on and clear space behind the treadmill. If you have a balance problem, ask your doctor or physiotherapist how to use one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to hold the handrails on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if you need them for balance. Holding on tightly or leaning on the rails makes the walk easier and changes your posture, so the effort and calorie figures will be lower than displayed. A light touch for balance avoids most of that, and many people need the rails less as their confidence grows.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill for seniors with handrails?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill is our pick for most older walkers, for its full-length rails, 0.3 mph start and simple controls. For taller walkers, the Redliro has a 47.6-inch belt. Our separate roundup of the best treadmills for seniors also covers full-size machines with low step-up heights and longer warranties.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you add handrails to a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We would not recommend it. Treadmill rails are part of the frame's design and are fixed at points the maker has engineered to take the load. An improvised rail may not be strong enough or may sit at the wrong height. If you need long rails, buy a treadmill designed with them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad with a handle as good as a treadmill with handrails?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for support. Most walking pads with a handle have one fold-up bar at the front, which helps you steady yourself when facing forward but gives nothing to hold at your sides. A treadmill with long side rails supports you along the whole belt, which matters more for walkers who are less steady.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
