        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying a Treadmill With Handrails</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these leads to a treadmill that does not give the support it was bought for.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mistaking console handles for long rails</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most treadmills have short handles near the console, and listings sometimes call them handrails. Check the photos: long rails run back along both sides of the belt, so you can hold on wherever you are standing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the starting speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">For someone unsteady, the first second on a moving belt matters most. A treadmill that starts at 0.3 mph and changes in 0.1 mph steps is gentler than one that starts at 0.6 mph or jumps in larger steps.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not clipping on the safety key</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A safety key only stops the belt if it is attached to your clothing. Clip it on every time, before you start the belt, and keep the space behind the treadmill clear in case you need to step off.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping advice when recovering from injury</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill with rails can be a good part of recovery, but speed, duration and how much to hold on vary from person to person. Ask your doctor or physiotherapist before starting, and follow their guidance on how fast to progress.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

